Requirements: 8   with obligations: 8

[task-01] A project whose tests live in a subdirectory with their own `pyproject.toml`
    and virtualenv has its tests run by `acceptance check`, using the project's own
    dependencies, and the tests pytest collects are recognised as the tests the tool
    asked for.
    -> subdir-tests-run-by-acceptance-check  [functional/explicit]
       A project whose tests live in a subdirectory with their own `pyproject.toml` and
       virtualenv has its tests run by `acceptance check`.
    -> use-project-dependencies  [functional/explicit]
       Use the project's own dependencies when running the tests.
    -> pytest-collected-tests-match-requested-tests  [invariant/explicit]
       The tests pytest collects are recognised as the tests the tool asked for.

[task-02] To make that possible, the user tells the tool once, per repository, the
    command they already use to run their tests — for example `cd api &&
    .venv/bin/python -m pytest` — and the tool runs the project's tests with exactly
    that command, adding only which tests to run. When no test command is set and the
    tests cannot be run, the tool says that a test command needs to be set, rather than
    reporting that pytest declines the tests.
    -> test-command-provided-once-per-repo  [functional/explicit]
       The user tells the tool once, per repository, the command they already use to run
       their tests.
    -> run-tests-with-configured-command  [functional/explicit]
       The tool runs the project's tests with exactly the configured command, adding
       only which tests to run.
    -> test-command-required-message  [functional/explicit]
       When no test command is set and the tests cannot be run, the tool says that a
       test command needs to be set.

[task-03] Tests run this way run as they do on the user's own machine: with the user's
    environment variables and with network access, so a suite that needs a local
    database and a variable such as `DATABASE_URL` can run.
    -> run-tests-with-env-and-network  [compatibility/explicit]
       Tests run as they do on the user's own machine, with the user's environment
       variables and with network access.
    -> local-db-and-env-var-test-runs  [functional/explicit]
       A suite that needs a local database and a variable such as `DATABASE_URL` can
       run.

[task-04] Because the tool runs the tests many times, including against deliberately
    broken copies of the code, setting the test command shows the user that risk in
    plain words and saves the command only after the user explicitly agrees. If the
    saved command changes, the user is asked to agree again. Each run prints the test
    command it uses and the names of the environment variables passed to the tests; it
    never prints their values. The tool never runs two copies of the project's tests at
    the same time.
    -> risk-warning-in-plain-words  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Setting the test command shows the user that the risk is described in plain
       words.
    -> save-command-only-after-explicit-agreement  [functional/explicit]
       The test command is saved only after the user explicitly agrees.
    -> reconfirm-on-command-change  [functional/explicit]
       If the saved test command changes, the user is asked to agree again.
    -> print-test-command-each-run  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Each run prints the test command it uses.
    -> print-env-var-names  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Each test run prints the names of the environment variables passed to the tests.
    -> hide-env-var-values  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Each test run prints the names of the environment variables passed to the tests,
       while hiding their values.
    -> single-test-run-at-a-time  [invariant/explicit]
       The tool never runs two copies of the project's tests at the same time.

